utionary  properties  the way this approach generally works is  that you create a  library of mutant variants of some  protein of interest and so in this case  rbd we're going to be creating libraries  in which we introduce all possible amino  acid changes across this 200 amino acid  protein  what makes the stimutational scanning  different than just classic biochemistry  is that instead of  then you know characterizing each of  these proteins one by one which  obviously does not scale to these large  numbers  um you actually pool all of these  mutants together and put them  through some sort of experimental assay  in which you can characterize each of  these mutations kind of  individually but in parallel and so in  this case we're going to be  characterizing the effects of all these  mutations  on two properties first of all sort of  the intrinsic folding stability  of the rbd itself as well as its  affinity for human h2 receptor  and so at the end of this experiment  what you end up with is sort of a map  of all possible amino acid changes  across this protein  with regards to their functional effects  and so this is just a snippet of data  i'll be showing you later  um just to kind of preview the way this  data works is each you know row  is a position in the rbd so site one two  three  um and then each uh row sorry columns  are sites each row  is an amino acid change and so for  example this blue square  for introducing arginine at the second  position indicates that this arginine  mutation  um endows for example higher affinity  for h2  whereas a position like this cysteine  here um in the fifth position  um with all these red squares indicate  that all these mutations away from  cysteine  are decreasing whatever functional  property is shown here  so that brings me to kind of the  specifics of what we did  here in this current study um in which  we undertook one of these deviational  scans  for the stars cov2 receptor binding  domain  so i'll start by explaining in a little  bit more detail the specifics of our  experimental assay and then we'll go  through some of the observations we made  from this data  including what it teaches us um a little  bit about the structure function  relationship of how this  receptor binding domain works um we'll  look a little bit at  how these maps of mutational effects can  help us to understand  currently circulating genetic variation  with the massage cov2 pandemic  and then finally i'll touch on what our  maps can teach us about  the epitopes on rbd that are targeted by  neutralizing antibodies  all right so um to kind of do this deep  mutational scan in a high throughput  setup  we turned to a classic system from  protein  engineering and biochemistry which is  beast surface display  so yeast surface display is a technique  that allows you to express  some protein of interest on the cell  surface tethered to individual yeast  cells  and so in this case we have yeast cells  which have  receptor binding domain multivalently  expressed on their cell surface  and when it's there we can do two key  things we can use fluorescent detection  of a tag on the rbd itself  to probe how well expressed the rbd is  on an individual yeast cell surface  and then beyond that we can incubate  with soluble h2  which is also fluorescently tagged so we  can monitor how well any given rdd  variant  binds to ace2 so to kind of show what  this looks like for example if we just  take the stars kobe 2  rbd and express it in its wild type form  in yeast  we can then do a titration series across  an increasing concentration  range of ace2 and we can monitor the  amount of ace2 binding  via flow cytometry and so what you can  see here is that as we increase the  concentration of h2  we get sort of a dose response curve of  h2 labeling of our yeast cells  and so from this relationship between  binding and concentration  we can fit these titration curves um  which are sort of these classic  biochemistry  curves from which we can compute the  dissociation constant or the kd which is  a measurement of the strength  of biochemical interaction between in  this case the wild type stars cov2  rbd and human h2 ligand  so to transition to doing this in more  of a high throughput format  um we first needed to create a mutant  library of stars ob2 so we can  characterize the effects of all these  mutations on this h2 ligand affinity  as well as the expression level of the  rbd and the cell surface  and to do that we constructed duplicate  mutant libraries  using a pcr-based codon mutagenesis  technique  which yielded us libraries of mutant  variants  um we also appended these barcode  sequences downstream  from the individual variant rbd genes  um and then we can put this system uh of  this library of mutant variants into our  yeast display system  but in contrast to what i showed you  before um instead of it just being a  single genotype  each individual yeast cell in the  population will now have  a different rbd variant on its cell  surface  um to kind of allow us to see what's  happening in an individual cell we use  these barcodes then  um what we do is we use long read path  biosequencing to link  individual barcodes to the actual mute  variants that they're associated with  and then what that means is for any  downstream experiment we can just do  short read illumina sequencing with the  barcodes  so for example if we read this yellow  barcode we know it represents this  individual single  mutant genotype  um and then i'm showing you here just  one table  illustrating the kind of coverage that  we get in these libraries  and if you look at the second column you  can see that in each of our duplicate  libraries  we have very high coverage we get  virtually every single possible amino  acid  change the rbd present in our libraries  i think we're missing only 15 of the  almost 4 000 different amino acid  changes that are possible to make  within this domain so next we wanted to  