riment  we have the titration curve for each of  these variants sorted independently  you can see how each genotype is in the  library is able to have its own  kind of variant specific titration curve  from which we can compute this  dissociation constant  and therefore end up with a measurement  of the effect of each amino acid on this  phase two binding affinity  and then as our last sort of  experimental kind of  measurement beyond simply determining  mutation effects on ace2 binding  as i alluded to before we're also  measuring the effects of these mutations  on the rbd expression level which is the  correlate of sort of the folding  stability  of the rbd um and so this is a similar  technique it's facts coupled with  sequencing but instead of doing it  across an entire dose response  range we're just doing it you know one  time at one labeling  of rbd expression levels um so we sort  cells into  bins based off of their level of  labeling for rbd expression level  and then we use sequencing to figure out  you know which genotypes are  you know higher or more highly or more  lowly expressing rbd  on the cell surface all right so that's  the technique  um there's a whole lot of qc that goes  into this and we also did a lot of  validation which i'm not going to get  into today  but i do encourage you to check out the  paper  to see kind of uh the reliability of  these measurements  um but what we end up with are these  heat maps as i showed you have very  small snippets of earlier  um illustrating the effects of all amino  acid changes  at each position in the rbd on these two  biochemical phenotypes  so on the top two panels are all the rbd  sites  sort of annotated by the mutation  effects on expression  that sort of stability metric and then  on the bottom  are all of these mutation effects on the  ace2 binding affinities  and so obviously these are very detailed  maps  and i don't expect you to  necessarily take away too much from  looking at them quickly from 