idues are  important for packing at the east 2  surface which is why they have this  binding specific constraint  all right so this has been focusing  mostly on constraint um but i also did  show you that there are many  kind of other interesting enhancing  mutations with respect to stability and  binding  and so i'm going to focus first on  stabilizing mutations and in particular  we were motivated by this idea that  there are a lot of people who want to  engineer rbd protein to have  certain properties such as  immunogenicity or  stability and these maps might be useful  for that and so here's one example of  the recent preprint  which is using an rvd-based vaccine  where  it displays these rbd proteins on the  surface of nanoparticles  another kind of rbd based tool are these  molecular probes  that people can use to isolate these  cells or antibodies that target  the rbd of um2  and we figured our maps could be useful  in two regards here um  we could be making rbd proteins that are  just better behaved biochemically for  example more stable or highly expressing  but beyond that as we learn more about  for example antibody epitopes  on the rbd our maps could be useful for  resurfacing  these rbd proteins um to for example  hide epitopes where perhaps we don't  want antibody responses  or to make better molecular probes to  isolate specific antibodies  so um to look at this a little bit more  i'm showing once again the map just for  mutational effects on expression  i mean in particular we were compelled  by this one region here kind of  down by amino acids 355 to 385 where we  see many blue mutations the mutations  that enhance stability  and so once again this is of course our  yeast display assay so we did want to  validate whether these  really have a stabilizing effect and so  in collaboration with dan ellis and neil  king  we cloned individual appointments of the  tsar's ob2 rbd  and expressed them in mammalian cells  and they saw that  these individual mut