otein dynamics and  when it's in this open conformation  um it exposes a surface um a contact  surface which  confers specificity to binding uh for  this ace2 receptor  so the h2 receptor is the protein on  host target cells  that the virus binds to and that sets  off  you know a chain reaction of  conformational changes which eventually  allow  the virus to enter the cell and initiate  cellular infection  and so because of sort of the key  importance of this receptor binding  domain  interaction with h2 we really wanted to  get  more insight into how the rbd or this  receptor binding domain  functions and confers specificity for  ace2  this rbd ace2 interaction has a lot of  interesting evolutionary  and immunological consequences uh so a  lot of prior work from  the original stars chronovirus epidemic  um  as well as ongoing work currently uh  shows that  rbd specificity for h2 has a crucial  role in cross-species transmission  so for example you can take bat viruses  which are unable to infect human cells  and if you you know transplant just this  receptor binding domain from a human  virus onto these back viruses  it allows them to then infect human  cells or similarly  adapting sarge covi2 for example to  replicate in mice select specific amino  acid changes  only within the receptor binding domain  also because of the key interaction of  this rbd with ace2  um many potently neutralizing antibodies  that are known to inhibit starch  coronavirus to  replication specifically target the rbd  and prevent its ability to bind to ace2  and so because of these key sort of  evolutionary and immunological  properties  um we wanted to use a standard technique  in the bloom lab  to study protein function specifically  within this rbd  and that technique is called  demutational scanning so mutational  scanning  is a experimental technique to  characterize large numbers of mutations  to a protein of interest  and thereby to learn more about sort of  its biochemistry and evolutionary  properties  the way this approach generally works is  that you create a  library of mutant variants of some  protein of interest and so in this case  rbd we're going to be creating libraries  in which we introduce all possible amino  acid changes across this 200 amino acid  protein  what makes the stimutational scanning  different than just classic biochemistry  is that instead of  then you know characterizing each of  these proteins one by one which  obviously does not scale to these large  numbers  um you actually pool all of these  mutants together and put them  through some sort of experimental assay  in which you can characterize each of  these mutations kind of  individually but in parallel and so in  this case we're going to be  characterizing the effects of all these  mutations  on two properties first of all sort of  the intrinsic folding stability  of the rbd itself as well as its  affinity for human h2 receptor  and so at the end of this experiment  what you end up with is sort of a map  of all possible amino acid changes  across this protein  with regards to their functional effects  and so this is just a snippet of data  i'll be showing you later  um just to kind of preview the way this  data works is each you know row  is a position in the rbd so site one two  three  um and then each uh row sorry columns  are sites each row  is an amino acid change and so for  example this blue square  for introducing arginine at the second  position indicates that this arginine  mutation  um endows for example higher affinity  for h2  whereas a position like this cysteine  here um in the fifth position  um with all these red squares indicate  that all these mutations away from  cysteine  are decreasing whatever functional  property is shown here  so that brings me to kind of the  specifics of what we did  here in this current study um in which  we undertook one of these deviational  scans  for the stars cov2 receptor binding  domain  so i'll start by explaining in a little  bit more detail the specifics of our  experimental assay and then we'll go  through some of the observations we made  from this data  including what it teaches us um a little  bit about the structure function  relationship of how this  receptor binding domain works um we'll  look a little bit at  how these maps of mutational effects can  help us to understand  currently circulating genetic variation  with the massage cov2 pandemic  and then finally i'll touch on what our  maps can teach us about  the epitopes on rbd that are targeted by  neutralizing antibodies  all right so um to kind of do this deep  mutational scan in a high throughput  setup  we turned to a classic system from  protein  engineering and biochemistry which is  beast surface display  so yeast surface display is a technique  that allows you to express  some protein of interest on the cell  surface tethered to individual yeast  cells  and so in this case we have yeast cells  which have  receptor binding domain multivalently  expressed on their cell surface  and when it's there we can do two key  things we can use fluorescent detection  of a tag on the rbd itself  to probe how well expressed the rbd is  on an individual yeast cell surface  and then beyond that we can incubate  with soluble h2  which is also fluorescently tagged so we  can monitor how well any given rdd  variant  binds to ace2 so to kind of show what  this looks like for example if we just  take the stars kobe 2  rbd and express it in its wild type form  in yeast  we can then do a titration series across  an increasing concentration  range of ace2 and we can monitor the  amount of ace2 binding  via flow cytometry and so what you can  see here is that as we increase the  concentration of h2  we get sort of a dose response curve of  h2 labeling of our yeast cells  and so from this relationship between  binding and concentration  we can fit these titration curves um  which are sort of these classic  biochemistry  curves from which we can compute the  dissociation constant or the kd which is  a measurement of the strength  of biochemical interaction between in  this case the wild type stars cov2  rbd and human h2 ligand  so to transition to doing this in more  of a high throughput format  um we first needed to create a mutant  library of stars ob2 so we can  characterize the effects of all these  mutations on this h2 ligand affinity  as well as the expression level of the  rbd and the cell surface  and to do that we constructed duplicate  mutant libraries  using a pcr-based codon mutagenesis  technique  which yielded us libraries of mutant  variants  um we also appended these barcode  sequences downstream  from the individual variant rbd genes  um and then we can put this system uh of  this library of mutant variants into our  yeast display system  but in contrast to what i showed you  before um instead of it just being a  single genotype  each individual yeast cell in the  population will now have  a different rbd variant on its cell  surface  um to kind of allow us to see what's  happening in an individual cell we use  these barcodes then  um what we do is we use long read path  biosequencing to link  individual barcodes to the actual mute  variants that they're associated with  and then what that means is for any  downstream experiment we can just do  short read illumina sequencing with the  barcodes  so for example if we read this yellow  barcode we know it represents this  individual single  mutant genotype  um and then i'm showing you here just  one table  illustrating the kind of coverage that  we get in these libraries  and if you look at the second column you  can see that in each of our duplicate  libraries  we have very high coverage we get  virtually every single possible amino  acid  change the rbd present in our libraries  i think we're missing only 15 of the  almost 4 000 different amino acid  changes that are possible to make  within this domain so next we wanted to  kind of do these titrations but in high  throughput  and so what i showed you before once  again was just doing a titration for  sort of an isogenic yeast population  um but as i was alluding to in the last  slide now we have a mixed  library population in which each yeast  cell has a different genotype on its  cell surface  and so if we look at this great dose  response curve here we no longer have  just a single response curve because  each genotype in the library is sort of  responding to  the ace2 labeling concentration um on  its own sort of  dissociation constant driven saturation  curve  and so rather than just monitoring  fluorescence via full cytometry  what we do for the library experiment  now is we use fluorescence activated  cell sorting  or facts which is a flow cytometry based  method that allows you to not only read  out fluorescence but also separate cells  on the basis of their porcelain signal  and so what we can do is that each of  these h2 concentrations  actually sort the cells into these four  bins illustrated by the dotted lines  and then we use aluminum sequencing to  read out which  use genotype is found in each of these  bins across each of these phase two  concentrations  and so for example um a genotype like  perhaps the yellow which has  high affinity for h2 will begin to kind  of fall into the bin two  three and four partitions at you know  relatively moderate h2 concentrations  i mean that might be different from a  genotype like the blue genotype which  perhaps  does not bind h2 which might be found in  bin one  across this entire concentration range  and so when we do this sort of you know  facts coupled with deep sequencing what  we end up getting  are these titration curves but for each  individual variant in the library  in parallel or simultaneously so what  i'm showing here on the right are just a  random selection  of genotypes in our library which are  labeled on the top  um and then from this kind of massively  parallel experiment  we have the titration curve for each of  these variants sorted independently  you can see how each genotype is in the  library is able to have its own  kind of variant specific titration curve  from which we can compute this  dissociation constant  and therefore end up with a measurement  of the effect of each amino acid on this  phase two binding affinity  and then as our last sort of  experimental kind of  measurement beyond simply determining  mutation effects on ace2 binding  as i alluded to before we're also  measuring the effects of these mutations  on the rbd expression level which is the  correlate of sort of the folding  stability  of the rbd um and so this is a similar  technique it's facts coupled with  sequencing but instead of doing it  across an entire dose response  range we're just doing it you know one  time at one labeling  of rbd expression levels um so we sort  cells into  bins based off of their level of  labeling for rbd expression level  and then we use sequencing to figure out  you know which genotypes are  you know higher or more highly or more  lowly expressing rbd  on the cell surface all right so that's  the technique  um there's a whole lot of qc that goes  into this and we also did a lot of  validation which i'm not going to get  into today  but i do encourage you to check out the  paper  to see kind of uh the reliability of  these measurements  um but what we end up with are these  heat maps as i showed you have very  small snippets of earlier  um illustrating the effects of all amino  acid changes  at each position in the rbd on these two  biochemical phenotypes  so on the top two panels are all the rbd  sites  sort of annotated by the mutation  effects on expression  that sort of stability metric and then  on the bottom  are all of these mutation effects on the  ace2 binding affinities  and so obviously these are very detailed  maps  and i don't expect you to  necessarily take away too much from  looking at them quickly from a bird's  eye view  but they are a super rich kind of  display of  a lot of important information and so  one  key thing we wanted to achieve with this  project was to make sure that  um any researcher doing who knows what  about sarge ob2  that might be able to leverage this  information um also has the ability to  kind of engage with these heat maps  um and so i'll flash up the link here um  but it's also present in the paper  we made interactive versions of these  heat maps so anybody can  kind of open them up on their own and  gaze at them for  hours and hours just like i did to kind  of emerge with some key insights  but just to kind of give some of this  bird's eye view  i'll focus on a few patterns that we can  see in these heat maps  um so in these heat maps as i said  before red  indicates mutations which are  deleterious for a phenotype such as  stability or binding  and then white indicates mutations that  are neutral and blue are mutations that  enhance that biochemical property and so  kind of as you expect  from just classic protein biochemistry  most mutations do as rbd do indeed  you know destabilize the protein or  inhibit its ability to bind these two  but at the same time we do see many  positions where lots of mutations are  neutral um and perhaps even more  surprisingly there are quite a few  positions  where a lot of blue mutations are  cropping up which is to say mutations  that  would enhance either the stability of  the rbd or even its affinity for human  ace too  um and so we'll revisit some of those  specific mutations a little bit later on  uh beyond that we can also see  heterogeneity and the level of  constraint on different positions across  the rbd  so there's some positions where you know  any mutation is kind of more or less  tolerated the same  whereas some positions are highly  constrained where any mutation away from  the wild type amino acid is deleterious  for function  we can also see that you know if you  kind of squint your eyes the  positions that are kind of constrained  for expression and binding tend to  overlap  um although there are some regions uh  where there's kind of  increasing constraint for expression or  binding individually  so with that sort of bird's eye and view  um we'll move on to talk a little bit  about some more specific  um sort of findings that were  observations we made about  the relationship between sequence  structure and function within the starch  ob2 rbd  so here what i'm doing is um taking this  heat map um sort of the average  mutational tolerance of each position  and mapping it to the structure of the  rdd bound to ace2  and so um here we've annotated a few  different features of the rbd  we've annotated what we're calling the  core rbd which is the central beta sheet  folded core of the protein which is sort  of the scaffold on which this rbm or the  receptor binding motif  sort of loopy region sits uh beyond that  i'm also showing some disulphides in  yellow and then  we're showing the bound ace2 ligand in  dark gray  at the top and so each sphere on the rbd  here is colored by the average  mutational effect  on the stability of the rbd so sort of  as you'd expect  um a lot of the positions that are most  constrained with respect to mutational  effects on stability  are sort of buried in the core of the  protein there are these sort of internal  positions  where if you muck them up with mutation  you end up destabilizing the protein  we can do the same thing coloring these  spheres now by their mutational effects  on binding  and as i pointed out before we see some  overlap many of these kind of  buried core positions are also  constrained with  regards to their effects on binding but  beyond that we can see that many more of  these positions  that cluster up at the interface with h2  are  constrained with respect to binding as  you'd expect  so look at this in a little bit more  detail um i'm showing here now for each  site  the correlation between its sensitivity  to mutation with respect to binding and  expression  and so once again you can see that most  positions sort of follow a general trend  where if they're sensitive  for stability they also lose binding  and then of course you can see there are  some positions where they are not  particularly sensitive with respect  mutation effects on expression  but they are for binding and when we  look at these on the structure  these are indeed these positions that  cluster to the h2 interface  um and sort of interact with these two  hotspot residues on these two these two  licenses  um we actually saw a little bit kind of  more interesting uh pattern in this  regard um taking into the more extreme  where we see that there's actually at  some positions  sort of trade-offs between rotational  effects on stability  and binding so here i'm showing heat  maps  uh focused just on five positions that  illustrate these tradeoffs  and what you can see at each of these  bypositions is that many of the  mutations  on left-hand heat map have deleterious  effects on binding  even though they would have improving  effects on the stability or the  expression of the rbg  so one example of this is glycine 502  where any mutation severely hinders  binding even though many of these polar  amino acids  enhance expression and when you look at  the structure this makes sense  it's glycine 502 on the rvd is in very  very close structural proximity to this  important  beta hairpin on these two and there's  simply just not room  for a side chain to accommodate these  two bindings so even though putting a  side chain here might enhance stability  it just wouldn't work for h2 binding  for these other foresights we saw that  these are hydrophobic residues  that are exposed on the rbd surface and  so exposed hydrophobics are known to  destabilize proteins and contribute to  things like aggregation  so mutating these hydrophobic residues  would enhance expression  but these hydrophobic residues are  important for packing at the east 2  surface which is why they have this  binding specific constraint  all right so this has been focusing  mostly on constraint um but i also did  show you that there are many  kind of other interesting enhancing  mutations with respect to stability and  binding  and so i'm going to focus first on  stabilizing mutations and in particular  we were motivated by this idea that  there are a lot of people who want to  engineer rbd protein to have  certain properties such as  immunogenicity or  stability and these maps might be useful  for that and so here's one example of  the recent preprint  which is using an rvd-based vaccine  where  it displays these rbd proteins on the  surface of nanoparticles  another kind of rbd based tool are these  molecular probes  that people can use to isolate these  cells or antibodies that target  the rbd of um2  and we figured our maps could be useful  in two regards here um  we could be making rbd proteins that are  just better behaved biochemically for  example more stable or highly expressing  but beyond that as we learn more about  for example antibody epitopes  on the rbd our maps could be useful for  resurfacing  these rbd proteins um to for example  hide epitopes where perhaps we don't  want antibody responses  or to make better molecular probes to  isolate specific antibodies  so um to look at this a little bit more  i'm showing once again the map just for  mutational effects on expression  i mean in particular we were compelled  by this one region here kind of  down by amino acids 355 to 385 where we  see many blue mutations the mutations  that enhance stability  and so once again this is of course our  yeast display assay so we did want to  validate whether these  really have a stabilizing effect and so  in collaboration with dan ellis and neil  king  we cloned individual appointments of the  tsar's ob2 rbd  and expressed them in mammalian cells  and they saw that  these individual mut