ct  that many of these mutations would be  tolerated or enhanced affinity  particularly because many of these kind  of blue mutations  are actually hydrophobic mutations which  would be removing these polar contacts  entirely  and so i think that shows there's a lot  of both mutational but correspondingly  structural flexibility  in how this interface can be kind of  engineered or rearranged  and that's reflected in the evolutionary  record in which this  interface and this rbd in general is  actually quite variable  among kind of stars related chronovirus  isolates  all right um so next we wanted to know  whether we could use our maps to  understand  more about sort of the evolutionary  forces that are guiding search kobe 2  genetic variation  this current phase of the pandemic and  so this pandemic is  interesting from one regard in that we  have  kind of new genomic technologies that  have really come online and  really change the way that we can  understand genetic variation  in this emerging pandemic and so um i  think currently there's something like  75 000  individual stars kobe 2 genomes that are  available um  and so um back when we did this analysis  in may or something like 35 or 30  000 mutation spike sequences available  um we looked at what variation is  present in these sequences within the  rdd  and in general we see that rbd mutations  are still quite rare  among um sequenced stars cov2 isolates  but beyond that besides just seeing  their frequency we now also have  functional annotations of these  mutations effects  so what i'm showing here is um on the  y-axis the effects of mutations on  binding  and then we bind um mutations by the  number of times they've been observed on  this repository of sequence stars  cov2islets  and so what you can see is that for any  mutation that has been seen  you know more than a handful of times  there's this increasing bias  in what types of mutational effects are  present among implementation sets  so anything you know seen more than six  times has essentially zero effect  on binding and so we see that as  evidence for purifying selection in  which mutations that would decrease  binding  are being selected against and that's  why they're not being seen at high  frequency  among these sequence variants  beyond that as i pointed out to you we  have many affinity enhancing mutations  as well  and so these mutations are indeed  accessible to  the wild type virus you know they can be  accessed by single nucleotide mutations  um but they're not being fixed or even  seen  in natural stars ob2 circulating strains  and so we thought that was interesting  given that these mutations are available  but not being seen  we take that as um evidence that perhaps  there's not any selective pressure for  higher affinity for ace ii which is to  say perhaps  you know the virus is already sorted in  that goldilocks sweet spot for the  degree of affinity that it has for human  ace ii  um kind of consistent with this idea  that there aren't really strong  phenotypic consequences of mutations  that are currently circulating in the  rbd  um we did look at sort of the most  prevalent mutations  um in sequence stars cody two isolates  and so the top five prevalent mutations  are shown here  um and you can see once again that um  we're really not seeing any strong  biochemical consequences  for these mutations with respect to our  phenotypes of phase two binding  and expression with just the one  exception being the 367f mutation uh  which we do ascribe a large stability  enhancing effect  and this was indeed one of the mutations  that we validated  in the purified rdds and so we don't  know what the consequence of that is  for the actual virus but i do think that  it's quite interesting to note  um and perhaps it's worth following up  on  and then of course um i think you know  it feels like it's been a really long  time but it seems like we're actually  probably still in quite an early phase  of this pandemic at least from the  perspective of the virus and its  evolutionary dynamics  um and so as this virus continues to  circulate in the population  and begin to encounter things like  engine pressure um  and you know a less wide availability of  sustainable hosts  um it'll be interesting to see or  continue to surveil mutations that are  arising  um in the context of these maps  all right um and then last um i'll talk  about what our maps might be able to  teach us about antibody epitopes within  the rbd  so as i mentioned at the beginning the  rbd is a common target of  antibodies that neutralize stars cov2  um and these antibodies are just showing  a structural alignment of several of  them  sort of decorate a bunch of different  surfaces on the rbd  um and related to this question about  how it might evolve over time  one super important question that we  don't really know much about yet  um is whether this type of virus or  whether this virus will be able to  do this type of evolution similar to how  flu evolves  um to sort of turn over its antigenic  determinants over time  or whether it will be more sort of  antigenically stable such as a virus  like measles um so to begin to get some  insight into that  we were motivated by a paper that came  out also during the summer  which passages viruses in the presence  of monoclonal antibodies  and then sequenced to see what types of  mutations it was fixing  to escape these neutralizing antibodies  that they were prone to  and so even though these antibodies  target the regions that surround these  kind of mutationally constrained  contacts  phase two the sites at which the  antibody escape mutations were actually  accruing in um this study uh we're kind  of finding the mutationally tolerant  residues in this otherwise constrained  region  and the specific mutations that we're  fixing um shown on the right  as you'd expect have sort of biased more  neutral effects on h2 binding infinity  obviously because if yo